WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:02.520
(dramatic music)
(gun blasting)

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.310
- [Narrator] The US Military
has launched a new school

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.140
to train American Armed Forces

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.840
in how to counter the
emerging threat of drones

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.130
or what it calls Unmanned
Aircraft Systems or UAS.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.900
The first academy of its kind,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.650
The Joint Counter Small Unmanned
Aircraft Systems University

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.160
or JCU will train about
1,000 troops a year.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.780
- Warfare is changing very fast.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.860
This threat right here,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.808
this current threat with respect to UAS,

00:00:28.808 --> 00:00:30.412
it's the pace of your phone changing.

00:00:30.412 --> 00:00:32.330
(drone clicking)
(dramatic music)

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:33.480
(drone buzzing)

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.060
- [Narrator] This footage is
from a Ukrainian drone attack

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.550
carried out against Russian forces,

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.610
and this video is from a
Hamas drone attack in November

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.043
carried out against
Israeli forces in Gaza.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.310
The proliferation of small, cheap,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.080
commercially available drones

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.060
is transforming modern warfare

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.731
and this has not been
lost on the Pentagon.

00:00:53.731 --> 00:00:56.310
(gun clicking)
(gun blasting)

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.520
Colonel Moseph Sauda is
the director of the JCU.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.790
- The pace of the need is
outgrowing capacity right now,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.160
so we're trying to train
as many people as possible

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.400
and trying to grow as fast
as possible to fit that need.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.560
- [Narrator] Today, students at Fort Sill

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.480
are training on weapons systems to counter

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.390
small unmanned aircraft.
(gun blasting)

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.650
- Alright, so what we
have in front of us here

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.530
is what's known as the Smart Shooter,

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.270
what the name implies is what it does.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.090
Alright, so the Smart Shooter
itself is a smart optic

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.300
that is placed onto the M4 weapon system

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.430
to allow the soldier a better opportunity

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.720
to engage small UAS threats
that are very close.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.020
(dramatic music)
(gun blasting)

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.510
- [Narrator] Smartshooter
training does involve drones,

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.660
but students don't shoot directly at them.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:39.930
(gun blasting)

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.490
Instead, they shoot at balloons

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.250
dangling below the drones on a string.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.310
The JCU said, "Balloons
are more cost effective,

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.620
and thinks the high winds in Oklahoma

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:50.970
are a harder target to hit.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:52.863
(gun blasting)
- [Instructor] Nice.

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:53.820
- So the soldier would place the weapon

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.670
into operation the way they
do for any other situation.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.040
From that point there, you
would look through the optic,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.080
which would acquire the target for you,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.270
in which case the soldier
would pull the trigger

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.307
and the rest of is done
by the weapon system.

00:02:05.307 --> 00:02:07.650
(gun blasting)
- [Instructor] Nice.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.240
- [Narrator] The students
are also learning

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.420
how to use another handheld
system, the Dronebuster.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.470
- Whereas the Smart Shooter
utilizes the 556 round,

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.050
this is known as an
electronic attack system,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.640
so a soldier's taking this here

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.780
and they're pointed in a general direction

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.000
of the target that they see.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.710
And then a soldier placed in an operation

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.463
utilize various jamming means
to interdict that target.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.050
- [Narrator] The JCU says,
"These weapons systems

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.100
and others like them

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.890
are already in the hands of
US forces around the world."

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.680
- It's not just the Air Force anymore

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.890
that is using unmanned
aerial vehicles or drones,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.450
it is also ground forces,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.720
and every echelon have
different types of drones

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.710
that provide them

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.440
with intelligence, surveillance,
and reconnaissance,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.260
as well as targeting, and
sometimes strike capabilities.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.150
- [Narrator] Before the JCU was created,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.790
soldiers who trained on
anti-drone weapon systems

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.590
did so within their own branches.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.840
- The Joint Counter UAS University,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.790
the intent here is to standardize

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.860
and centralize the training

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.780
so that we can all have interoperability

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.120
as we work together and
integrate the system successfully

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.260
with respect to the threat

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.580
- [Narrator] Training typically lasts

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.260
between one to three weeks.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.390
Students are selected by their commanders,

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.160
but some volunteer to attend.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.620
- As far as branches of
Military coming through the JCU,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.960
we are seeing service
members from all branches.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.970
We also train federal agencies

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.920
as well as allied and partner nations.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:29.850
- [Narrator] In the years following

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.650
the September 11th attacks,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.430
the US deployed large,
sophisticated, expensive drones

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.940
for surveillance and strikes
in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.680
The first time commercial drones

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.900
were used in a conflict was in Iraq.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.170
When ISIS deployed the technology
in the battle for Mosul,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.600
- ISIS began to modify small quadcopters

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.080
and use them to attack
Iraqi and US forces.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.200
And this presented an
unprecedented challenge.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.280
- [Narrator] In recent years,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.740
civilian drones modified with explosives

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.240
have become a growing menace

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.600
for troops in Syria, Gaza, and Ukraine.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.880
- Drones are fundamentally
changing the way

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.530
that militaries fight.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.160
They are allowing different
states to perform missions

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.440
that previously were too expensive.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.590
Having an Air Force on
the cheap, for example.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.090
- [Narrator] Many of the small drones

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.100
being used in combat today in Ukraine

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.980
come from one Chinese company, DGI.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.530
DGI drones aren't made to fire weapons,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.420
but they can be easily modified

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.390
to carry a grenade or
other small explosive.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.227
Last year, DGI said,

00:04:32.227 --> 00:04:35.100
"It would stop selling its
drones to Ukraine and Russia

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.740
because the technology was
not intended to be used

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.270
for military activities

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.910
- In Ukraine, you've
seen just an explosion

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.400
of commercial and do-it-yourself drones

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.070
on a level that has
never been seen before.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.580
Both parties are employing
them at all echelons.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.680
The Ukrainians were the first movers here

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.460
and you've seen them move
into first-person view drones,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.900
these small, fast quadcopters
that are essentially kamikazes

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.520
and crash into their target.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.020
- [Narrator] Students at Fort Sill

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.320
are also training for an
emerging threat, drone swarms,

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.210
the term refers to a network

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.940
of multiple unmanned
aerial flying platforms

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.010
integrated together by a single system.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.590
- We have the ability to
replicate drone swarms here,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:17.670
and this is very important

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:18.720
because as a student,

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.970
you'll have a realistic appraisal

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.090
of the difficulties that these
different platforms present,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.700
but also you'll have an
understanding of the capabilities

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.240
of what you're utilizing
to neutralize that threat.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.230
- [Narrator] Given how rapidly

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.150
drone technology is developing,

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.260
the Pentagon believes

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.200
there's no single weapon
system that can be relied upon.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.150
- You're really in a cost competition

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.630
because so many of the
drones are very cheap today,

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.540
that you don't wanna be firing something

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.010
that is much more expensive

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.560
to intercept and destroy them.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.270
- There is no silver bullet

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.010
when it comes to counter UAS.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.410
So there is no one system
that is most effective.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.060
Commanders have to determine,

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.770
based off of planning factors,

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.140
which system is best to be
utilized against the threat.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:01.973
And so what we want to do

00:06:01.973 --> 00:06:03.990
is make sure we use a layered approach,

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.030
using multiple systems

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.430
planning against that in
different environments,

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.710
don't look for one way out.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.570
You want to create multiple dilemmas,

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.970
which then makes it difficult
for the adversary to develop

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.548
means to defeat what
you're putting together.

00:06:17.548 --> 00:06:22.548
(dramatic music)
(drone buzzing)

